==Seed dispersal==
[[Aril]]ated seeds of Marantaceae are [[Seed dispersal|dispersed]] mainly by birds and mammals. In [[Amazon rainforest|Amazonia]], crickets and ants are important secondary dispersers.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Santana|first1=Flávia Delgado|last2=Baccaro|first2=Fabricio Beggiato|last3=Costa|first3=Flávia Regina Capellotto|date=2016|title=Busy Nights: High Seed Dispersal by Crickets in a Neotropical Forest|journal=The American Naturalist|language=en|volume=188|issue=5|pages=E126–E133|doi=10.1086/688676|pmid=27788347|bibcode=2016ANat..188E.126S |s2cid=3925801|issn=0003-0147}}</ref>

==Phytochemistry==
[[Rosmarinic acid]] can be found in plants in the family Marantaceae such as species in the genera ''Maranta'' (''[[Maranta leuconeura]]'', ''[[Maranta depressa]]'') and ''[[Thalia (plant)|Thalia]]'' (''[[Thalia geniculata]]'').<ref>{{cite journal | doi = 10.1016/j.phytol.2008.09.010 | volume=1 | issue=4 | title=Occurrence of rosmarinic acid, chlorogenic acid and rutin in Marantaceae species | year=2008 | journal=Phytochemistry Letters | pages=199–203 | last1 = Abdullah | first1 = Yana| bibcode=2008PChL....1..199A }}</ref>

==Rapid plant movement: secondary pollination presentation==
Marantaceae have a distinctive pollination mechanism that is defined by an explosive style movement. It is commonly termed explosive because the action is swift, occurs only one time for each flower, and is irreversible.<ref name="Pischtschan-2008">{{Cite journal |last1=Pischtschan |first1=E. |last2=Claßen-Bockhoff |first2=R. |date=2008 |title=Setting-up tension in the style of Marantaceae |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1438-8677.2008.00051.x |journal=Plant Biology |language=en |volume=10 |issue=4 |pages=441–450 |doi=10.1111/j.1438-8677.2008.00051.x |pmid=18557904 |bibcode=2008PlBio..10..441P |issn=1435-8603}}</ref> This quick pollination event plays a significant role in optimizing mating and has been hypothesized to be a factor in the high level of speciation within this family.<ref name="Ley-2011" /> There are two parts of the floral anatomy that contribute to the explosive pollination mechanism: the style and the hooded staminode.<ref name="Ley-2011" />

The secondary pollination presentation begins after a mechanical stimulus is introduced by a pollinator on the trigger appendage of the hooded staminode.<ref name="Jerominek-2015">{{Cite journal |last1=Jerominek |first1=Markus |last2=Claßen-Bockhoff |first2=Regine |date=2015 |editor-last=Chen |editor-first=Zhong-Hua |title=Electrical Signals in Prayer Plants (Marantaceae)? Insights into the Trigger Mechanism of the Explosive Style Movement |journal=PLOS ONE |language=en |volume=10 |issue=5 |article-number=e0126411 |doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0126411 |doi-access=free |issn=1932-6203 |pmc=4440630 |pmid=25997015 |bibcode=2015PLoSO..1026411J }}</ref><ref name="Pischtschan-2008" /> Touching this trigger causes the release of the style from the contacted staminode, leading to rapid inward rolling of the style which is no longer being held under high tension.<ref name="Jerominek-2015" /><ref name="Claßen-Bockhoff-1991">{{Cite journal |last=Claßen-Bockhoff |first=Regine |date=1991 |title=Untersuchungen zur Konstruktion des Bestäubungsapparates von Thalia geniculata (Marantaceen); Investigations on the Construction of the Pollination Apparatus of Thalia geniculata (Marantaceae) |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1438-8677.1991.tb00215.x |journal=Botanica Acta |language=en |volume=104 |issue=3 |pages=183–193 |doi=10.1111/j.1438-8677.1991.tb00215.x |bibcode=1991BotAc.104..183C |issn=0932-8629}}</ref> During this quick rolling movement, self pollen (located on top of the style) is deposited on the pollinator while cross-pollen is scooped off the pollinator into the stigmatic cavity.<ref name="Claßen-Bockhoff-1991" /><ref name="Pischtschan-2008" /> This single action occurs very quickly with the full movement being clocked in at around 0.03 seconds.<ref name="Claßen-Bockhoff-1991" />

==Uses==
The most well-known species in the family is arrowroot (''[[Maranta arundinacea]]''), a plant of the [[Caribbean]], grown in parts of the Caribbean, [[Australasia]], and [[sub-Saharan Africa]] for its easily digestible starch known as [[arrowroot]]. It is grown commercially in the [[West Indies]] and tropical [[Americas]].{{Citation needed|date=July 2018}}

Several species of genus ''[[Goeppertia]]'' are grown as [[houseplants]] for their large ornamental leaves, which are [[Variegation|variegated]] in shades of green, white, and pink. Other genera grown for houseplants include ''[[Stromanthe]]'', ''[[Ctenanthe]]'', ''[[Calathea]]'', and ''[[Maranta (plant)|Maranta]]''.{{Citation needed|date=July 2018}}

''Calathea lutea'' has tough, durable leaves used to make waterproof baskets, and in the [[Caribbean]] and [[Central America]], its leaves are used for roofing. Two [[Mexico|Mexican]] species - ''[[Calathea macrosepala|C.&nbsp;macrosepala]]'' and ''[[Calathea violacea|C.&nbsp;violacea]]'' - have flowers that are cooked and used as vegetables. ''[[Calathea allouia|C.&nbsp;allouia]]'' or leren, from the West Indies and tropical America, is known as sweet corn root for its edible tuber.{{Citation needed|date=July 2018}}

''[[Schumannianthus dichotomus]]'' is used for weaving mats to use on floors and beds.{{Citation needed|date=July 2018}}

